12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail Norman Myers' criteria for global Biodiversity Hotspots, and characterize the four hotspots extending into India.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Norman Myers' Biodiversity Hotspot Framework and Detailed Profiles of India's Four Hotspots:) Tj
T*
(   1. Norman Myers' Dual Quantitative Criteria:) Tj
T*
(   To qualify as a global Biodiversity Hotspot, a biogeographic region must satisfy two strict) Tj
T*
(   criteria:) Tj
T*
(   - Criterion 1 \(High Endemism\): Must contain at least 1,500 species of vascular plants \(> 0.5%) Tj
T*
(   of world total\) as endemics.) Tj
T*
(   - Criterion 2 \(Extreme Threat\): Must have lost at least 70% of its original primary native) Tj
T*
(   vegetation cover \(retaining <= 30% intact primary habitat\).) Tj
T*
(   Currently, 36 global hotspots are recognized, representing only 2.4% of Earth's land surface) Tj
T*
(   but containing over 50% of world endemic plants.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Four Hotspots Extending into India:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Western Ghats and Sri Lanka:) Tj
T*
(   * Geography: Extends along the 1,600 km western coastal mountain spine from Gujarat to Kerala.) Tj
T*
(   * Endemism: Over 2,100 endemic flowering plants; 84% of amphibians and 62% of reptiles are) Tj
T*
(   endemic.) Tj
T*
(   * Key Species: Nilgiri Tahr, Lion-tailed Macaque, Malabar Civet, Purple Frog \(Nasikabatrachus) Tj
T*
(   sahyadrensis\).) Tj
T*
(   * Threats: Commercial plantations \(tea, coffee, rubber\), hydroelectric dams, iron ore and) Tj
T*
(   bauxite mining.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Eastern Himalayas:) Tj
T*
(   * Geography: Encompasses Arunachal Pradesh, Sikkim, Bhutan, and eastern Nepal.) Tj
T*
(   * Endemism: Abundant primitive flowering plant families \(Magnoliaceae\), Rhododendrons, and) Tj
T*
(   orchids.) Tj
T*
(   * Key Species: Red Panda, Snow Leopard, Golden Langur, Himalayan Musk Deer.) Tj
T*
