(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the tri-zonal architecture of Biosphere Reserves under UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere \(MAB\) Program, using the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   UNESCO MAB Biosphere Reserve Architecture and Case Study of the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conceptual Framework of Biosphere Reserves:) Tj
T*
(   Initiated in 1971 by UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere \(MAB\) Program, Biosphere Reserves are areas) Tj
T*
(   of terrestrial, coastal, and marine ecosystems designed to reconcile the conservation of) Tj
T*
(   biodiversity with its sustainable socioeconomic use.) Tj
T*
(   2. Tri-Zonal Spatial Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Core Area \(Strict Protection\):) Tj
T*
(   * Dedicated to long-term biodiversity preservation with zero human exploitation or settlement.) Tj
T*
(   * Legally protected as National Parks or Sanctuaries. Only non-destructive scientific) Tj
T*
(   monitoring and low-impact research are permitted.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Buffer Zone:) Tj
T*
(   * Delineated contiguous to or surrounding the core zone.) Tj
T*
(   * Accommodates compatible, ecologically sound activities: environmental education, regulated) Tj
T*
(   ecotourism, low-impact sustainable forestry, and agroforestry. Buffer zones protect the core) Tj
T*
(   from external edge pressures.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Transition Area \(Zone of Cooperation\):) Tj
T*
(   * The outermost flexible zone where local agricultural communities, scientists, civil society,) Tj
T*
(   and government agencies collaborate to manage sustainable farming, agro-pastoral livelihoods,) Tj
T*
(   and human settlements.) Tj
T*
(   3. Case Study: The Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve \(Established 1986\):) Tj
T*
