T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the Shannon-Wiener Diversity Index \(H'\), derive its mathematical bounds, and calculate diversity for a concrete ecological community.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mathematical Formulation and Derivation of the Shannon-Wiener Diversity Index \(H'\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Information-Theoretic Derivation \(Claude Shannon, 1948\):) Tj
T*
(   The Shannon-Wiener Index originates from information theory, measuring the degree of) Tj
T*
(   uncertainty in predicting the species identity of an individual randomly selected from an) Tj
T*
(   ecological community:) Tj
T*
(   H' = - Sum_{i=1}^{S} [ p_i * ln\(p_i\) ]) Tj
T*
(   Where:) Tj
T*
(   - S: Total number of species in the community \(Species Richness\).) Tj
T*
(   - p_i: Proportion of individuals belonging to the i-th species relative to total individuals:) Tj
T*
(   p_i = n_i / N.) Tj
T*
(   - ln: Natural logarithm \(to base e; log2 or log10 may also be used\).) Tj
T*
(   - The negative sign converts the negative logarithmic values into a positive diversity index.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Boundary Limits:) Tj
T*
(   - Minimum Diversity \(H'_min = 0\): Occurs in a completely mono-specific community \(S = 1\), where) Tj
T*
(   p_1 = 1.0: H' = - [ 1.0 * ln\(1.0\) ] = 0. There is zero uncertainty.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum Diversity \(H'_max = ln\(S\)\): Occurs when all S species are present in perfectly equal) Tj
T*
(   abundance \(p_i = 1/S for all i\):) Tj
T*
(   H'_max = - Sum [ \(1/S\) * ln\(1/S\) ] = - S * [ \(1/S\) * \(-ln S\) ] = ln\(S\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Pielou's Evenness Index \(J'\):) Tj
T*
(   J' = H' / H'_max = H' / ln\(S\)) Tj
T*
(   Ranges from 0 \(complete inequality\) to 1.0 \(perfect evenness across all species\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Solved Ecological Calculation:) Tj
T*
(   - A wetland community contains 4 bird species: Species A \(40 individuals\), B \(30 individuals\),) Tj
T*
ET

