12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the mathematical formulation of Simpson's Diversity Indices: Dominance Index \(D\), Gini-Simpson Index \(1-D\), and Reciprocal Index \(1/D\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Simpson's Diversity Formulations: Dominance, Gini-Simpson, and Reciprocal Metrics:) Tj
T*
(   1. Edward Simpson's Dominance Index \(D, 1949\):) Tj
T*
(   Simpson's Index measures the probability that two individuals randomly selected from a) Tj
T*
(   community belong to the exact same species:) Tj
T*
(   - For an infinite population: D = Sum_{i=1}^{S} \(p_i\)^2) Tj
T*
(   - For a finite sample: D = Sum_{i=1}^{S} [ n_i * \(n_i - 1\) ] / [ N * \(N - 1\) ]) Tj
T*
(   Where n_i is the number of individuals of species i, and N is the total count of individuals.) Tj
T*
(   - Properties of D:) Tj
T*
(   * D ranges from 0 to 1.0.) Tj
T*
(   * High values of D indicate high dominance by one or few species \(low diversity\).) Tj
T*
(   * Low values of D indicate low dominance and high diversity. Because D is inversely related to) Tj
T*
(   diversity, it is termed a 'Dominance Index.') Tj
T*
(   2. Transformational Metrics:) Tj
T*
(   To make the index increase monotonically with increasing biodiversity, two transformations are) Tj
T*
(   universally used:) Tj
T*
(   - Gini-Simpson Index \(1 - D\):) Tj
T*
(   \(1 - D\) = 1 - Sum \(p_i\)^2) Tj
T*
(   Represents the probability that two randomly selected individuals belong to different species.) Tj
T*
(   Ranges from 0 \(monoculture\) to 1.0 \(infinite diversity\).) Tj
T*
(   - Simpson's Reciprocal Index \(1 / D\):) Tj
T*
(   Inverse Simpson Metric = 1 / [ Sum \(p_i\)^2 ]) Tj
T*
(   Represents the 'effective number of species' in the community. Ranges from 1 \(a community) Tj
T*
(   containing only 1 species\) up to S \(when all species have equal abundance\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Comparative Application:) Tj
T*
(   Simpson's index is heavily weighted toward the most abundant species in a sample and is) Tj
T*
(   relatively insensitive to rare species, whereas Shannon's index is more sensitive to changes in) Tj
T*
(   rare species.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 18:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail MacArthur and Wilson's Equilibrium Theory of Island Biogeography, deriving the equilibrium species richness and turnover rate equations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   MacArthur and Wilson's Equilibrium Theory of Island Biogeography \(1967\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Conceptual Framework:) Tj
T*
(   Robert MacArthur and Edward O. Wilson proposed that species richness on an oceanic island \(or) Tj
T*
(   isolated habitat patch\) represents a dynamic equilibrium between two opposing biogeographical) Tj
T*
