(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the Arrhenius Species-Area Relationship, derive the linearized logarithmic equation, and quantify species loss from habitat fragmentation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Arrhenius Species-Area Relationship and Quantitative Extinction Modeling:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Power Model \(Olof Arrhenius, 1921\):) Tj
T*
(   Empirical ecological surveys demonstrate that species richness \(S\) scales non-linearly with) Tj
T*
(   contiguous sampling area \(A\):) Tj
T*
(   S = c * A^z) Tj
T*
(   Where:) Tj
T*
(   - S: Total number of species.) Tj
T*
(   - A: Geographic habitat area \(hectares or km2\).) Tj
T*
(   - c: Biogeographic taxon-specific constant \(reflecting intrinsic species density per unit) Tj
T*
(   area\).) Tj
T*
(   - z: Scaling exponent representing the rate of species accumulation with increasing area.) Tj
T*
(   2. Logarithmic Linearization:) Tj
T*
(   Taking logarithms of both sides converts the power function into a linear regression model:) Tj
T*
(   log\(S\) = log\(c\) + z * log\(A\)) Tj
T*
(   The slope of the regression line equals the exponent z:) Tj
T*
(   - Mainland Continents: z typically ranges from 0.12 to 0.18. Continental contiguous habitats) Tj
T*
ET

