(   [Problem Statement]: Examine Population Viability Analysis \(PVA\) and formulate the four stochastic extinction risks defined by Mark Shaffer.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Population Viability Analysis \(PVA\) and Mark Shaffer's Stochastic Extinction Risks:) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept of Population Viability Analysis \(PVA\):) Tj
T*
(   PVA is a rigorous computational modeling methodology that uses demographic, genetic, and) Tj
T*
(   environmental data to project the probability that a biological population will persist over a) Tj
T*
(   specified timeframe \(e.g., 99% probability of persistence for 1,000 years\). It identifies the) Tj
T*
(   Minimum Viable Population \(MVP\) required for long-term survival.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mark Shaffer's Four Stochastic Extinction Drivers \(1981\):) Tj
T*
(   Shaffer demonstrated that small populations are vulnerable to extinction through four distinct) Tj
T*
(   stochastic forces:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Demographic Stochasticity: Random fluctuations in individual birth and death rates and sex) Tj
T*
(   ratios. In a population of 10 rhinos, if all calves born in three consecutive years are male,) Tj
T*
(   reproductive continuity is severed.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Environmental Stochasticity: Unpredictable annual variations in weather, rainfall,) Tj
T*
(   temperature, food supply, and predator/pathogen populations that affect all individuals in the) Tj
T*
(   population simultaneously.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Natural Catastrophes: Extreme episodic events of catastrophic intensity \(hurricanes,) Tj
T*
(   volcanic eruptions, extensive forest fires, infectious pandemics like canine distemper in) Tj
T*
(   lions\) that can instantly eradicate an entire geographically isolated subpopulation.) Tj
T*
(   - 4. Genetic Stochasticity: Random loss of adaptive alleles via genetic drift, fixation of) Tj
T*
(   deleterious mutations, and cumulative inbreeding depression over generations.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Extinction Vortex:) Tj
T*
(   As population size drops, demographic, environmental, and genetic stochasticities reinforce) Tj
T*
(   each other in a downward spiral: lower population -> increased inbreeding -> reduced juvenile) Tj
T*
(   survival -> smaller population -> accelerated genetic drift -> ultimate extinction.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
