BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the regulatory mechanisms of CITES, contrasting the permit requirements and species listings of Appendices I, II, and III.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   CITES \(Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Historical Mandate and Legal Authority \(1975\):) Tj
T*
(   Drafted in 1973 under the auspices of the IUCN and entered into force in 1975, CITES is an) Tj
T*
(   international treaty comprising over 184 member state parties. It protects more than 38,000) Tj
T*
(   species of wild animals and plants against destructive overexploitation through international) Tj
T*
(   commercial trade.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Three Appendices and Permitting Regimes:) Tj
T*
(   - Appendix I \(Threatened with Extinction\):) Tj
T*
(   * Scope: Species threatened with extinction that are or may be affected by international trade) Tj
T*
(   \(approx 1,000 species, e.g., Tiger, Snow Leopard, Asian Elephant, Mountain Gorilla, Sea) Tj
T*
(   Turtles\).) Tj
T*
(   * Rule: Commercial international trade is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.) Tj
T*
(   * Exceptions: Trade permitted only under exceptional non-commercial circumstances \(scientific) Tj
T*
(   research, conservation breeding\).) Tj
T*
(   * Permitting: Requires BOTH an Import Permit from the destination country's Management) Tj
T*
(   Authority AND an Export Permit from the origin country, certifying that the transfer is) Tj
T*
(   non-detrimental to wild survival.) Tj
T*
(   - Appendix II \(Controlled Commercial Trade\):) Tj
T*
(   * Scope: Species not currently threatened with extinction, but which may become so unless) Tj
T*
(   commercial trade is strictly regulated \(over 37,000 species, including American Black Bear, all) Tj
T*
(   orchids, Cactaceae, mahogany\). Also includes 'look-alike' species to ensure effective border) Tj
T*
