(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the Community Forest Resource \(CFR\) rights under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers Act \(FRA, 2006\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Forest Rights Act \(FRA, 2006\) and Community Forest Resource \(CFR\) Governance:) Tj
T*
(   1. Historical Context and Legislative Mandate:) Tj
T*
(   Enacted in 2006 to rectify the 'historical injustice' committed against forest-dwelling) Tj
T*
(   indigenous communities \(Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers - OTFDs\) whose) Tj
T*
(   ancestral customary tenure rights were unrecorded during colonial and post-colonial state) Tj
T*
(   forest reservation.) Tj
T*
(   2. Core Typologies of Rights Recognized:) Tj
T*
(   - Individual Forest Rights \(IFR, Section 3\(1\)\(a\)\): Legal title to self-cultivated forest land) Tj
T*
(   \(up to a maximum of 4 hectares\) occupied prior to December 13, 2005.) Tj
T*
(   - Community Rights \(CR, Section 3\(1\)\(b\)\): Rights of ownership, collection, use, and disposal of) Tj
T*
(   Non-Timber Forest Produce \(NTFP, e.g., Tendu leaves, Mahua flowers, bamboo, lac, wild honey\),) Tj
T*
(   and traditional grazing rights in water bodies.) Tj
T*
(   - Community Forest Resource \(CFR\) Rights \(Section 3\(1\)\(i\)\):) Tj
T*
(   The landmark right to 'protect, regenerate, or conserve or manage any community forest resource) Tj
T*
(   which they have been traditionally protecting and conserving for sustainable use.') Tj
T*
(   3. Decentralized Gram Sabha Governance:) Tj
T*
(   - The Act democratizes forest administration by recognizing the Gram Sabha \(village general) Tj
T*
(   assembly\) as the primary statutory authority to initiate the process of determining forest) Tj
T*
(   rights.) Tj
T*
(   - Section 5 empowers the Gram Sabha to constitute a village Forest Protection Committee to) Tj
T*
(   regulate access to CFRs, halt destructive industrial mining or logging, and conserve local) Tj
T*
(   biodiversity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
