(   - The 'Escalator to Extinction':) Tj
T*
(   * High-altitude alpine species already adapted to cold mountane summits \(e.g., Snow Leopard,) Tj
T*
(   Himalayan Pika, alpine gentians\) have nowhere higher to migrate.) Tj
T*
(   * As sub-alpine woody trees and lower-elevation generalist competitors advance uphill, the) Tj
T*
(   physical land area of alpine habitats shrinks toward the mountain peak.) Tj
T*
(   * Summit species suffer severe range contraction, genetic isolation, and ultimate ecological) Tj
T*
(   extinction \('mountaintop trap'\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Trophic Phenological Mismatch:) Tj
T*
(   - Climate warming alters the seasonal timing \(phenology\) of key life-history events \(bud burst,) Tj
T*
(   insect emergence, migratory bird egg-laying\) at asymmetric rates across trophic levels.) Tj
T*
(   - Empirical Example: Long-distance migratory insectivorous birds time their spring arrival to) Tj
T*
(   historical photoperiod cues, arriving at breeding grounds at the same calendar date. However,) Tj
T*
(   local warming causes caterpillar prey populations to emerge 2-3 weeks earlier. When bird chicks) Tj
T*
(   hatch, caterpillar abundance has already peaked and collapsed, resulting in mass chick) Tj
T*
(   starvation.) Tj
